# State Management

Use the state-management solution already adopted by the project.

Do not introduce a second state-management framework for a feature without a strong architectural reason.

Prefer explicit states such as Initial, Loading, Success, Empty, and Error.

Avoid representing complex state using multiple unrelated booleans that permit impossible combinations.

Keep state at the lowest level that needs to own it. Do not move local UI state into global application state without a reason.

Keep side effects outside pure UI rendering.
